WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.420
Speaker 1: It is great to have Rick Sanchez
joining us today, veteran journalist and Emmy

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:10.130
Award winner, who is the co-founder, CEO and
president of Agua Media.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.490
Rick, it's so great to have you on.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:18.920
Speaker 3: Listen, David, I am so proud of
you as a fellow Latino.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.539
Everything you do, your determination, your
ability to always fight for the little guy.

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:28.060
Your consistency, general fairness, your messaging.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:31.240
You do a great job, David, and you ought to
be commended.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:35.950
And I just got to be told I got to tell you,
with all transparency, I've been a fan for

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:36.950
some time.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:38.580
I've been listening to what you do and you
do a really great job.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.780
Speaker 1: So I'm shocked and flattered.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:48.210
But also it provides like a really good segue
way into when we think about the dynamics

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:55.120
of the American Spanish speaking or Latino
origin community there.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.400
It's such a diverse community.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.320
Many people see me.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.609
They say, David, you look white.

00:01:01.609 --> 00:01:03.940
How is it that you're South American?

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:08.470
And of course, you and I and many people in
our community, we know about the diversity.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:11.960
But politically, it's also quite a diverse
group.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:18.830
And as as I want to hear from you, the conventional
wisdom is often Cubans and Venezuelans.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:24.460
They often come to the U.S. They become Republicans
because they see that as a way to fight that

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:28.100
which they left in their home countries.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:33.390
Those from many other South American countries
and Central American tend to be more Democratic

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.390
leaning.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:36.690
Is that the is that still accurate today?

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.140
Speaker 3: I think to a certain extent it
is.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.340
Unfortunately, the it's overplayed.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.310
So what happens is, like with the election,
with Trump, for example, Trump did, in fact,

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:49.540
you know, he gets his he gets credit for winning
Florida.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.649
Unfortunately, he didn't win Nevada.

00:01:51.649 --> 00:01:52.759
He didn't win Texas.

00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:54.700
He didn't win anywhere else in the country.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.600
70% of the people in the United States are
not Venezuelans or Argentineans or Cubans.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.860
They are, for the most part, either Mexican
or from other parts of Central America.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.990
But that message gets lost, David.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:13.910
So still, the message seems to be that all
the Latinos are moving to the Republican camp.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.650
And that's not true.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:21.569
Latinos continue to vote basically two thirds
Democrat and one third Republican.

00:02:21.569 --> 00:02:24.150
And that's changed a little bit over time.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:28.040
But it seems to me that the message that's
coming certainly from the right, the right

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:34.160
is spending millions and millions of dollars
with Americano and all these other front groups

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.860
to try to bring in and Soros, for example,
to try and bring in Latinos.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:43.500
They get credit for trying, but considering
the amount of money they're putting into it

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:49.900
and considering how little competition they're
getting from Democrats to try and keep Latinos

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:51.849
there, they actually aren't doing that.

00:02:51.849 --> 00:02:53.800
Well, I wouldn't score them highly on that.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:54.800
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.030
Speaker 3: Mean during those two things I
just.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:03:02.019
Speaker 1: Said it's interesting because you
often touted it is, oh, in 2020, Trump increased

00:03:02.019 --> 00:03:05.370
his share of the Latino vote by 8%.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:10.849
But when you start with a small number even
increasing, it is not super impressive.

00:03:10.849 --> 00:03:14.159
But that being said, if the trend continued,
it actually would be huge.

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:15.159
Hugely significant.

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:16.690
Speaker 3: Well, sure.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:21.600
And they even did well in this last election
if you want to go by decimal points.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:22.600
Yeah.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:27.010
And I think to a certain extent, look, I mean,
let's just call this what it is.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:31.840
How much can Latinos be told that they're
rapists and criminals by certain segments

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:36.280
of the Republican Party, like Cruella de Vil
running for governor in Arizona?

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:42.209
And, you know, after a while, it's obviously
going to create a backlash.

00:03:42.209 --> 00:03:48.220
The problem and I wish what I would see is
more David Pakman and more Rick Sanchez is

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:51.230
and I'm not just trying to say that we're
the only ones that got this thing figured

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:56.870
out, but I'd like to see that on the NBCs
in the CBS is in the CNN and other places

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:01.910
where they don't just kowtow to Latinos and
tell you about how much they like you, but

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:06.060
actually understand that we're the fifth largest
GDP in the world.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:14.220
If we were a country that 80% of us are American
citizens, that 30, 40, 41% of us under the

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:18.550
age of 50 speak English as our language.

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:20.230
None of these things are proliferated.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:27.680
So I don't I think both the Republican Party
beaten the crap out of the Latinos and then

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:33.650
the Democratic Party, unfortunately, not going
far enough to really represent or understand

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:34.650
Latinos.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.850
Almost even taking them for granted to a certain
extent creates this dynamic that we're in

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:38.850
right now.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.140
And it's got to change on both sides, obviously.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:44.370
Speaker 1: One of the stories that's been
told in the political world for at least ten

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:48.700
years, if not even longer, is there's these
demographic shifts happening in states like

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:49.990
Florida and Texas.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:54.740
And part of it is age related, but a lot of
it is the growth of the Hispanic or Latino

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:59.240
population, and it's just going to make it
difficult and eventually impossible for Republicans.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:02.080
To win Florida and Texas, and it hasn't played
out.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:05.360
And many people are looking at the numbers
and saying, okay, is this happening?

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:06.360
Is it?

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.139
Is it going to play out?

00:05:08.139 --> 00:05:09.380
What is happening with that?

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:14.000
Speaker 3: Well, I think you have to take
one thing into account, and this is maybe

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.490
the most important thing.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:23.350
Latinos, the common age of a Latino, David,
is 11 on, say, 11.

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:24.389
That's the note, right?

00:05:24.389 --> 00:05:29.070
Scientifically speaking, mathematically speaking,
the common age of a Latino is 11 like that

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:34.729
number in because the common age of a white
Anglo-Saxon non-Latino in the United States

00:05:34.729 --> 00:05:36.889
is 58.

00:05:36.889 --> 00:05:42.610
So what that tells you is because Latinos
are young, they're going to be voting in the

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:43.610
future.

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:48.250
More in line with what we just saw, some young
people voting in this last election.

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:52.940
So what they're going to care about is not
communist, the communists like we hear today

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:58.470
in parts of south Florida, but they're probably
going to be voting more in line with regular,

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:01.419
you know, American young people.

00:06:01.419 --> 00:06:03.670
And that would not bode well for the Republicans.

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:08.999
But right now, yeah, Republicans have done
a great job in South Florida, in particular,

00:06:08.999 --> 00:06:13.190
bringing in the Venezuelan and the Cuban vote
and the others who come from certain parts

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:17.361
of Latin America by telling them that if you
vote Democrat, you're voting for Chavez, you're

00:06:17.361 --> 00:06:21.060
voting for Fidel Castro because all Democrats
are communists.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:26.130
Speaker 1: One of the things that applies
generally when it comes to targeting young

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:31.849
voters is that the right seems much more committed
financially to setting up things like Turning

00:06:31.849 --> 00:06:37.090
Point USA, sending Ben SHAPIRO to college
campuses and paying handsome speaking fees,

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:38.090
etc.

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:41.490
You really don't see it as much on on the
left.

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:46.380
And it seems that the same thing applies specifically
to the targeting of Latino voters, where there's

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:53.080
lots of right wing Spanish language political
content on the radio in the United States,

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:56.370
but really not much alternative to that.

00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:01.379
Speaker 3: You know, that's kind of one of
the reasons I'm doing Iowa Media, because

00:07:01.379 --> 00:07:06.890
it's really a bit of a passion project for
myself and for Solomon Trujillo, my partner,

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.030
you know, saw.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:14.590
And I realized that if we don't get the story
out ourselves and thank God we have a David

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:19.569
Pakman who's also a Latino, who's also an
influential member of the media community,

00:07:19.569 --> 00:07:26.331
to be able to tell the story that unfortunately,
I wish MSNBC would tell, but they dodge, let's

00:07:26.331 --> 00:07:30.400
face it, we're 20% of the population in the
United States and we are underrepresented

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.990
across the board, whether it's Hollywood or
whether it's on cable television.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:38.300
Nancy Pelosi said something interesting.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:40.010
God love her and I know she made a mistake.

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:44.610
And she's an older woman and stuff, but she
should have known better soon after DeSantis

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:49.120
did this stunt where he literally hijacked
people or kidnaped people and sent them to

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:50.720
another state from Texas.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:57.330
Her response was, What a shame, because imagine
the farmers in Florida could have used some

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:02.430
of those immigrants, for example, to pick
their fruit and vegetables.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:03.810
And I myself, Nancy, God bless you.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:07.729
But that's not what you say the answer is
right now.

00:08:07.729 --> 00:08:10.810
And in the future, Latinos will be the doctors,
the business people.

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:16.259
They'll be the people who create this unbelievably
enterprising economy of ours.

00:08:16.259 --> 00:08:17.319
They tend to hire more.

00:08:17.319 --> 00:08:19.569
They tend to start more small businesses.

00:08:19.569 --> 00:08:23.240
You need to know that, Nancy, when you're
talking about Latinos, and that's the problem,

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:27.410
and we don't get it at CNN, we don't get it
at the MSNBC scenes.

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:29.580
And those are the ones who are supposed to
be our stalwarts.

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.909
And in that example and it's not the beat
up, you know, Nancy Pelosi.

00:08:32.909 --> 00:08:35.750
But yeah, you know, that's that's the wrong
message.

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:38.390
That's the wrong counter to the other argument.

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:39.390
That's important, David.

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:42.419
And that's why you and I, guys like you and
I are out there to share these things.

00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:46.320
Speaker 1: Well, one thing I want to ask you
about, and I have no idea what your position

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:48.560
is on this, so I could be stepping into a
minefield.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:54.720
Here is one of the things that when you talk
about the conversation among the left and

00:08:54.720 --> 00:09:00.350
Latino Americans has to do with the gender
neutralizing of the Spanish language.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:06.810
And I'm talking about Latin X and I'm so that's
in English, Latin X and then there's a movement

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:13.529
in Spanish as well where it would take something
like the word amigos or amigos, which are

00:09:13.529 --> 00:09:18.790
gendered and create a new one, which is a
me guess with an E now.

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:21.760
My view is this is a really bad idea.

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:24.490
I don't think this makes any sense.

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:28.510
Most people in Central and South America don't
use that.

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:34.339
Later example I gave Latin X is mostly being
proposed by white Americans.

00:09:34.339 --> 00:09:39.390
I think that this if this becomes the thing
that is the topic in the U.S. among the left

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:41.730
with this population, I think it's a disaster.

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:42.730
Where are you on that?

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:49.149
Speaker 3: We're just throwing the right a
bone to be able to use to coalesce Latinos

00:09:49.149 --> 00:09:50.829
against Democrats.

00:09:50.829 --> 00:09:55.480
And then they could go with that woke thing
that, you know, is so amorphous and opaque.

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:57.280
I don't even know what the hell it means.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:59.390
But yeah, don't do that.

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:03.890
I'm so proud of my mother for everything she
gave to me, as you are of your mother.

00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:05.690
And your mother is a Latina.

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:09.230
And my mother is a Latina and my father is
a Latino.

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:13.530
I can't think of describing Paco, my dad,
as a Latin ex, you know.

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:15.450
What does that mean to me?

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:19.279
Besides, if you're going to do it with us
Latinos, then you also have to do it with

00:10:19.279 --> 00:10:24.600
Italians because they do use a non gender
neutral, you know, language, because they

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:29.640
all come from romance languages and Romanians
and the French and Portuguese and everything,

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:31.790
so leave well enough alone.

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:36.070
As far as our friend Willie Shakespeare said,
when it comes to something like this and let's

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:40.380
tackle the really big issues that are affecting
Latinos in the United States and not change

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:42.010
their nomenclature.

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:43.649
It just seems like a bridge too far.

00:10:43.649 --> 00:10:46.339
It almost seems like almost a little insulting
to me.

00:10:46.339 --> 00:10:51.950
So when it comes to Latin X, in fact, when
it comes to all colorations, I don't think

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:53.110
we should be brown.

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:54.590
I don't think we should be yellow.

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:56.310
I don't think we should be black.

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:59.860
I think you're an Argentinean American and
I am a Cuban-American.

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:03.760
And my friend Jerry over here, who I'm looking
at is a Mexican-American.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:07.149
And somebody can be a Jamaican American or
an Irish American.

00:11:07.149 --> 00:11:08.790
It's a better description.

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:11.960
In the old days when you called someone a
colored person, it was insulting.

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:16.079
And it seems like today we want to bring that
back and somehow celebrate it.

00:11:16.079 --> 00:11:17.410
I'm against colors.

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:19.700
Let's call each other as descriptively as
we can.

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:23.600
As we learned in journalism school so that
we understand how people are described.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:28.880
Speaker 1: Let's talk a little bit about immigration
and specifically Dhaka.

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:36.750
Dhaka, to me, seems like it would be the lowest
hanging fruit that both Democrats and Republicans

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:42.380
would be incentivized to figure out and deal
with and say, okay, here's something we've

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:43.470
fixed.

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:46.370
So it's not just endlessly a, quote crisis.

00:11:46.370 --> 00:11:51.650
Now, of course, the term crisis is exaggerated
and it's used, you know, right.

00:11:51.650 --> 00:11:52.800
Leading up to an election.

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:54.510
And then suddenly you don't hear about it
for a year.

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:55.959
We know all of that stuff.

00:11:55.959 --> 00:11:56.959
Okay.

00:11:56.959 --> 00:12:03.389
Yeah, but on the DOCA issue, it seems overwhelmingly
popular to say we're not going to send people

00:12:03.389 --> 00:12:10.670
back to other countries who came here through
no fault of their own as minors with parents.

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:11.970
Why hasn't it happened?

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:17.680
Is part of it that neither side wants to allow
the other side to be able to take credit for

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:18.680
that?

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:24.760
Speaker 3: I think, to be honest with you,
obviously, Republicans have had an odd and

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:28.930
almost cruel view of that and have for many,
many years, with some exceptions.

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:33.950
You know, we can give some credit to George
Bush and we can give some credit hell even

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:35.420
to Lindsey Graham at some point.

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:36.980
I don't know what the heck happened to that
guy.

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:41.640
There was a time when he had a different way
of thinking about things like this.

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:48.541
Yeah, but unfortunately, Democrats have never
really made this, including, you know, President

00:12:48.541 --> 00:12:55.079
Obama have never really made this the priority
that they that they needed to make it to to

00:12:55.079 --> 00:13:02.339
to really make sure that they were, you know,
making strides with the Latino community where

00:13:02.339 --> 00:13:03.779
it matters, you know, right here.

00:13:03.779 --> 00:13:09.810
And I think, you know, as we like to say and
that's, you know, I think to a fault.

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:16.100
And I think oftentimes Democrats, you know,
as I said in that famous and that's in that

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:21.520
famous documentary that Billy did, you know,
the thing about Democrats is they're always

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:24.370
trying to do the right thing and please everybody.

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:27.250
And Republicans could give a crap they just
want to win.

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:31.750
So Republicans don't care who they insult
and what they do as long as in the end they

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:32.750
can win.

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:35.519
That's kind of the McConnell message, right,
in many ways, which it's interesting now that

00:13:35.519 --> 00:13:39.820
they're trying to kick him out and Democrats
instead have always kind of overplayed things

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:42.420
and been concerned about how it's going to
be seen.

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:47.240
And well, if we come out too heavy on this,
then we could lose middle America because

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:49.640
we might lose votes in the Midwest.

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:52.450
And they overthink things so much and in the
end, they don't do enough.

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:58.070
And I think if Democrats had gotten on board,
Dakota, for example, really got on board,

00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:03.420
I think they would be faring better now and
not fighting, you know, between 60, 65 and

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:05.459
70% of the vote.

00:14:05.459 --> 00:14:09.320
And really, they would probably be topping
out around 80% because remember, the people

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:14.341
that are affected by this especially are the
people who are the larger part of the Latino

00:14:14.341 --> 00:14:19.360
community, the Mexican American and the Central
American part of those Latino communities.

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:21.170
So it would make a huge difference, I think
will.

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:24.600
Speaker 1: Hopefully we will at least see
some progress on that at some point.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:31.730
It may take Democrats allowing Republicans
to do it under their watch to get that win.

00:14:31.730 --> 00:14:35.331
And the question is whether Democrats would
go for it because it's the right thing to

00:14:35.331 --> 00:14:37.899
do even if they don't get all of the political
benefit.

00:14:37.899 --> 00:14:39.019
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:14:39.019 --> 00:14:40.019
Yeah.

00:14:40.019 --> 00:14:43.519
You know, it's funny, in this last election,
I watched something happen.

00:14:43.519 --> 00:14:46.430
There was a guy who turned out to be a pretty
awful human being.

00:14:46.430 --> 00:14:48.190
But for some reason, he and I used to talk
a lot.

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:51.360
He would call me at least once a week when
he was running Fox News, and that's Roger

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:52.360
Ailes.

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:57.000
And he used to say to me and said publicly,
in fact, that the funny thing about Republicans

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:59.100
is they don't tell you what they.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:05.920
Think they just show up to the polling place
and show their actions with their vote and

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:07.889
they don't go around giving their opinions.

00:15:07.889 --> 00:15:12.661
Now, what a difference between what just happened
in this election, not just with Latinos, but

00:15:12.661 --> 00:15:14.450
with others.

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:17.360
Republicans are now the hippies of the 1970s.

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:19.130
They're the ones having parades.

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:20.720
They're the ones putting it out there.

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:23.940
I mean, I would never go screaming down the
middle of the street, holding a banner of

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:28.630
any candidate or putting bumper stickers all
over my house and doing all of those things.

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:32.870
And that's what you see now with with with
Republicans.

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:34.459
And you invite them over to your backyard
barbecue.

00:15:34.459 --> 00:15:37.329
And he happens to be your neighbor and he's
Republican and immediately starts telling

00:15:37.329 --> 00:15:39.750
you who who's going to vote for who he hates.

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:42.769
And you're like, okay, here, want a beer?

00:15:42.769 --> 00:15:43.769
It's the weirdest.

00:15:43.769 --> 00:15:44.780
It's the weirdest thing.

00:15:44.780 --> 00:15:45.780
It's strange.

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:47.699
Why are you telling me this?

00:15:47.699 --> 00:15:53.209
And that used to be what Democrats were kind
of like in the sixties and seventies.

00:15:53.209 --> 00:15:55.649
Now Democrats just keep their mouths shut.

00:15:55.649 --> 00:15:56.649
They wait.

00:15:56.649 --> 00:15:59.040
And when it comes to vote, they voted against
it.

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:01.490
And that's kind of what we saw in this last
midterm.

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:05.730
And it made me think of the flip that maybe
happened in our society.

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:06.730
Yeah.

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:10.880
And Roger Ailes captured the quiet Republican
and created a television network for them.

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:11.880
Right.

00:16:11.880 --> 00:16:14.701
And brought them out to the point now where
they're I guess they're loud and proud, but

00:16:14.701 --> 00:16:15.701
not effective.

00:16:15.701 --> 00:16:16.701
Speaker 1: Right.

00:16:16.701 --> 00:16:18.310
They're not quiet anymore, that's for sure.

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:19.569
Speaker 3: No, no.

00:16:19.569 --> 00:16:20.990
I mean, that's the point.

00:16:20.990 --> 00:16:24.290
It's almost like it's counterintuitive or
ironically different.

00:16:24.290 --> 00:16:29.480
Speaker 1: We've been speaking with Rick Sanchez,
who's the co-founder, CEO and president of

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:30.480
Agua Media.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.670
Rick, really a pleasure having you on.

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:34.980
Speaker 3: It's a pleasure to be with you
as well.

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:35.980
David, thanks so much.

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:38.290
And you know, by most ballot.

